building libstdoc
=================

Requirements
------------

  * CMake 3.16 or newer.
  * GNU C or Clang (the flags are GNU-style and are only used after
    the compiler has been identified).
  * An aarch64 Linux target.  Any other operating system or
    architecture is rejected at configure time rather than silently
    mapped onto an implementation that does not match it: the build
    fails with a message that names what is missing.
  * Optionally a C++ compiler for the C++ compatibility test.  When
    none is found, that test is skipped and everything else builds.

No network access, no package manager and no third-party dependency
is needed at any point.


Quick start
-----------

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

Builds are out-of-source only: "build" is the conventional directory
name and is already ignored by Git.


Build options
-------------

STDOC_BUILD_TESTS
    ON by default.  Set to OFF to build only the library:

        cmake -S . -B build -DSTDOC_BUILD_TESTS=OFF

STDOC_HEAP_SIZE
    Size in bytes of the dynamic memory arena (4096 by default).  It
    reaches the allocator as a compile definition, so the configured
    value is the only one the library is built with; it reaches the
    test programs the same way, so none of them is built against a
    different value from the library it links.

    The smallest arena that can work is 64 bytes: every block carries a
    header of its own, and an arena that cannot hold one block and the
    payload of another can serve no program at all.  The configuration
    guard rejects anything below 64, and anything that is not a plain
    decimal number, when the project is configured rather than when it
    runs.

    cmake -S . -B build -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=1024

    The tests do not depend on the value: they ask the allocator what
    it has and shape each scenario to fit, so the same sources pass from
    64 bytes upwards, and a smaller heap only makes a leak show up
    sooner.


How the flags are chosen
------------------------

The library is compiled as freestanding code and the tests are not,
for reasons that matter:

    library    -std=c17 <warning set> -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
               -fno-stack-protector

    tests      -std=c17 <warning set> -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector

    link       -nostdlib -static

  * "-ffreestanding" is deliberately not used for the test programs:
    it makes clang treat main() as an ordinary function, mangles its
    name and removes the implicit "return 0", so the program would
    exit with whatever the last call left in the return register.
  * "-fno-builtin" stops a call from being rewritten into a compiler
    builtin that this library does not provide.
  * "-fno-stack-protector" removes the need for a failure handler
    symbol that does not exist here.
  * "-nostdlib -static" makes the library's own _start, its printf and
    its string helpers the only ones available at link time, which is
    the point of the whole exercise.  "-static" is also what keeps the
    result a plain non-PIE executable: both drivers already imply
    "-no-pie" for a static link, and passing it as well only makes
    Clang report it as an unused argument on every link.

The same warning set is used for the library and for the tests, so a
test can never be sloppier than the code it exercises:

    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror
    -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
    -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes -Wcast-qual
    -Wundef -Wswitch-default -Wdouble-promotion -Wvla

Every one of them is understood by both GNU C and Clang, so no check is
made before they are passed.  The two prototype options only exist for
C, so the C++ test is built without them, which GNU C++ requires.  Two
test programs add "-Wno-format" to the set for a reason that is written
down where it applies; see docs/testing.

Every C file in the project, tests included, is built as C17 with
extensions off, so no target is ever compiled against a dialect the
library does not support.


Installation
------------

The default prefix is /usr/local, which normally needs the privileges
of the root user:

    cmake -S . -B build
    sudo cmake --install build

A per-user installation needs no privileges and puts everything below
the home directory:

    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cmake --install build

The prefix belongs on the configure line, not on the install line:
the paths written into libstdoc.pc are fixed when the project is
configured, so "cmake --install build --prefix ..." moves the files
but leaves the metadata pointing at the configured prefix.

Four things are installed: the archive, the two headers, a CMake
package and a pkg-config file.


Consuming the library
---------------------

With pkg-config:

    cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libstdoc)

A prefix outside the search path of pkg-config, such as the home
directory, is not looked in by default:

    PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/.local/lib/pkgconfig" \
        cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libstdoc)

With CMake:

    find_package(libstdoc REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(my_program PRIVATE libstdoc::stdoc)

pointing CMake at the prefix with -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<prefix>.  The
imported target carries the include directory with it.

Note that the library is freestanding by design: a program linked
without a C runtime needs the entry point that libstdoc provides, and
one linked with a normal C runtime gets its entry point from there
instead.

One detail of linking without a C runtime is worth knowing before it is
met the hard way, because the symptom - the program dies with SIGBUS
before main() has done anything - says nothing about the cause.  GNU ld
places the read-write segment at a file offset congruent to its virtual
address modulo the maximum page size of the target, and it pads the file
out to that offset only when it has something to write there.  A static
executable with nothing file-backed in its read-write segment - no
global object with an initializer, nothing the linker has to write into
the image - therefore has that segment placed past the end of the file,
the kernel maps the page from there, and the first access to the .bss
that follows it is reported as SIGBUS.  One initialized object anywhere
in the program is enough to prevent it, and a program that uses
stdoc_stdout or stdoc_stderr already has several.  Linking with
"-z max-page-size=4096" avoids it as well, at the cost of executables
that a kernel with 64 KiB pages may refuse to load; the test suite
prefers the initialized object.  test/largewrite.c records the same
thing where it applies.


Continuous integration
----------------------

.github/workflows/ci.yml runs on an aarch64 runner - the target this
library implements, so nothing there is cross-compiled - and checks
three things on every push and pull request:

  * the build and the test suite under GNU C and under Clang, at the
    default arena size and at the smallest one the configuration
    accepts, so the boundary the allocator documents is exercised as
    well as the ordinary case;
  * test/expected-format.txt regenerated from the host C library, which
    fails if the golden file in the repository no longer matches what a
    reference library prints, so it cannot drift into being a snapshot
    of libstdoc;
  * the configuration guard, which has to reject the arena sizes it
    documents as impossible.

Every step the workflow runs can be run by hand with the commands in
this document and in docs/testing.


Porting
-------

The platform-specific pieces are chosen in exactly two places, and a
new operating system or architecture has to satisfy both:

  1. CMakeLists.txt decides what is supported.  It currently accepts
     Linux on aarch64 only and fails with a clear message for
     anything else, so an unimplemented target can never silently
     pick an incompatible implementation.

  2. The implementation itself:
       assembly/start_aarch64.S  program entry point,
       assembly/syscall.S        system-call trampoline,
       io/syscall_linux.c        system-call numbers and conventions.

A port adds the files for its target, teaches the CMake guard about
them, and keeps every operating system or architecture specific value
out of the portable code in io/ and mem/.  The system-call layer is
the only place where numbers and register conventions appear.

Cross-compilation is not claimed to work: it needs a toolchain for
the target and a successful build before anyone can say it does.
